      14  32        37            100  2.1386  -0.0825
      15  34        41            100  2.3654   0.0664
\end{Verbatim}

\subsubsection*{Interpretation of the printed summary}

The summary above the chosen crosses describes the breeding scenario,
objective and numerical qualification of the plan.
\begin{description}
\item[Criterion and status.] The criterion names the objective.
\cde{best.found} denotes a feasible search result, whereas \cde{optimal}
also requires a bound certifying the optimum. \cde{search.limit} means
that the search has not found a feasible plan.
\item[Families and progeny.] These give the number of distinct crosses,
the progeny count per family and the total progeny count.
\item[Expected mean.] This is the average predicted cross mean for these
equal-sized families. With supplied cross scores, expected usefulness
additionally includes the predicted gain from within-family selection;
\cde{accuracy} specifies the assumed accuracy of that ranking.
\item[Group relatedness.] This is $c'Kc$ for the pooled parental gene
shares, compared with the requested limit.
\item[Average within-cross relatedness.] This is the average kinship
between the two parents of each chosen cross. It concerns pairing and
can change while pooled parental contributions remain fixed.
\item[Objective bound and optimality gap.] The bound is an upper bound
for a maximized objective and a lower bound for the minimized pairing
objective. The gap measures the remaining difference from that bound.
\item[Maximum constraint residual.] This is the largest numerical
violation of the plan constraints.
\end{description}
The cross table gives the parental identifiers, progeny counts, predicted
cross means and parental kinships. A usefulness column appears when the
corresponding cross scores are supplied.

\subsection{Pairing at fixed parental contributions}

With \cde{mode="fixed"}, the parental gene shares are supplied explicitly.
Their additive predicted mean and group relatedness remain fixed, while
the pairing objective is to minimize average within-cross relatedness. This example
uses the gene shares of the preceding feasible plan, so they already
correspond to integer parental-use counts for the 15 families. Other
supplied proportions that cannot be realized by the requested integer
family counts are reported as infeasible without automatic rounding.
The preceding plan provides the starting mating list through
\cde{control=list(start=cross.plan)}.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
fixed.plan <- st.cross.plan(
    merit = cross.merit,                       # Parental genetic values
    K = cross.K,                               # Kinship matrix
    n.crosses = 15,                            # Number of distinct parent pairs
    n.progeny = 100,                           # Progeny per family
    theta = 0.02,                              # Group relatedness limit
    upper = 0.1,                               # Maximum parental contribution
    contributions = cross.plan$contributions,  # Fixed parental gene shares
    mode = "fixed",                            # Pairing at fixed contributions
    control = list(                            # Search settings
        start = cross.plan                     # Starting crossing plan
    )
)
fixed.plan
@
