\subsection{The usefulness criterion}

The usefulness criterion maximizes the average cross mean plus
\cde{accuracy} times the expected selection gain, \cde{es - mu}.
The supplied value of one represents perfect within-family ranking under this
fitted additive model. An accuracy of zero reduces the objective to the
same mean criterion. The score computation, constraints and default search
budget are identical in the two calls.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
usefulness.plan <- st.cross.plan(
    merit = cross.merit,                       # Parental genetic values
    K = cross.K,                               # Kinship matrix
    n.crosses = 15,                            # Number of distinct parent pairs
    n.progeny = 100,                           # Progeny per family
    theta = 0.02,                              # Group relatedness limit
    upper = 0.1,                               # Maximum parental contribution
    scores = cross.scores,                     # Precomputed cross scores
    criterion = "usefulness",                  # Objective: maximum usefulness
    accuracy = 1,                              # Within-family ranking accuracy
    control = list(                            # Search settings
        start = cross.plan                     # Starting crossing plan
    )
)
usefulness.plan
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
