Both results report the predicted population mean and usefulness, making
the trade-off visible for the two feasible crossing lists. Their
\cde{best.found} status describes the search result rather than a certified
global optimum. The group-relatedness limit still concerns nominal
parental contributions before within-family selection. It does not specify
the genomic ancestry of the retained descendants.

The same crossing commands apply to \cde{usefulness.plan\$crosses}.
These expected selected-descendant scores
describe the fitted additive model and the declared selection scenario;
they do not establish an advantage in field performance.

\section{Optimum mate allocation}

Optimum mate allocation assigns a continuous fraction of the produced
progeny to each allowed cross (Endelman, 2025). The parental contributions
follow from those mating fractions. This differs from the preceding optimum
cross-selection examples, which specify 15 distinct families with 100 progeny
each. Continuous mate allocation has no fixed number of families or integer progeny counts.

\subsection{Mating and parental bounds}

The following analysis uses the same complete founder panel, kinship matrix
and full-precision cross scores. The three columns of \cde{oma.parents}
identify each parent and its minimum and maximum gene contribution.
The mating table identifies both parents, the cross mean to be optimized,
and its allocation bounds. Here each parent and each mating can receive
at most one tenth of the corresponding total.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
oma.parents <- data.frame(id=rownames(cross.K), min=0, max=0.1)
oma.mean.matings <- data.frame(
  parent1=cross.scores$P1Name, parent2=cross.scores$P2Name,
  merit=cross.scores$mu, min=0, max=0.1)
@

\subsection{Mean-based mate allocation}

\cde{base="RM"} uses the randomly mated reference, whose value is the mean
of all entries in the supplied \cde{cross.K}. The two \cde{dF} values
specify an interval for the first-generation average within-mating kinship,
called \cde{F1}. The upper endpoint also sets the bound on the
second-generation group quantity, \cde{F2}. On this centered reference,
the reference value is approximately zero, so \cde{dF=c(0,0.01)} bounds
\cde{F1} between zero and 0.01 and \cde{F2} by approximately 0.0199.
These are a chosen reference-scale scenario, not absolute probabilities
of identity by descent or a recommended biological inbreeding rate.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
oma.mean <- st.oma(
    parents = oma.parents,                     # Parent contribution bounds
    matings = oma.mean.matings,                # Allowed crosses and scores
    K = cross.K,                               # Kinship matrix
    dF = c(0, 0.01),                           # F1 change range; F2 change cap
    base = "RM"                                # Random-mating reference
)
oma.mean
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
