The returned allocations are continuous fractions. An operational crossing
plan additionally needs integer family and progeny counts satisfying its
own constraints. These fractions are not rounded automatically and are
not an integer family plan for the simulation in
Section~\ref{sec:planned-dh}. The optimum cross-selection interface remains the corresponding choice when the
requested result is a list of equal-sized integer families.

\section{Proportional loss of expected genetic standard deviation}
\label{sec:propsd}

The published PropSD criterion of Fern\'andez-Gonz\'alez et al.\ (2026)
describes proportional loss of expected genetic standard deviation relative
to a fixed reference population. The expectation follows a Gaussian genetic
model with covariance proportional to the supplied matrix. Its implementation
uses exact moments of sample variance and a two-moment Gamma approximation
to expected sample standard deviation. This quantity differs from the
standard deviation of the fitted parental merits and from group relatedness.
Appendix~\ref{app:cross-mathematics} gives the mathematical definitions.

The complete 30-parent matrix defines the reference sample here. Each
family contributes two parental occurrences to the selected sample, including
repeated occurrences of a parent used in several families. Such occurrences
represent the same genetic random variable. They are not independent copies.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
parent.slots <- c(usefulness.plan$crosses$P1Name,
                  usefulness.plan$crosses$P2Name)
diversity.loss <- propsd(
    G = cross.K,                               # Complete reference covariance
    parents = parent.slots                     # Parental occurrences in families
)
diversity.loss
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
Proportional loss of expected sample SD
PropSD: 0.0256
Expected SD: reference 1.0044; selected 0.9788
Sample occurrences: reference 30.0000; selected 30.0000
Gaussian moments; two-moment Gamma approximation
\end{Verbatim}

The loss is one minus the selected-to-reference ratio of approximate expected
standard deviations. A value of 0.03 represents a 3\% loss under this model;
negative values are possible when the selected sample has greater expected
variation. The reference remains fixed when the selected occurrences change.
The reported approximation does not describe realized ancestry or variation
after selection within the descendant families.

\subsection{A diversity constraint in cross selection}

The optional diversity list constrains the same numerical estimate during
cross selection. The explicit \cde{family.slots} declaration specifies the
sample convention used above. The complete matrix can also contain reference
parents outside the eligible crossing panel. The objective remains usefulness
and the existing contribution and group-relatedness limits still apply.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
diversity.plan <- st.cross.plan(
    merit = cross.merit,                       # Parental genetic values
    K = cross.K,                               # Kinship matrix
    n.crosses = 15,                            # Number of distinct parent pairs
    n.progeny = 100,                           # Progeny per family
    theta = 0.02,                              # Group relatedness limit
    upper = 0.1,                               # Maximum parental contribution
    scores = cross.scores,                     # Precomputed cross scores
    criterion = "usefulness",                  # Objective: maximum usefulness
    accuracy = 1,                              # Within-family ranking accuracy
    diversity = list(                          # Additional diversity constraint
        G = cross.K,                           # Complete reference covariance
        max = 0.025,                           # Strict upper limit on PropSD
        sample = "family.slots"                # Two parental occurrences per family
    ),
    control = list(                            # Search settings
        starts = 4,                            # Number of search starts
        maxit = 5000                           # Proposals per search start
    )
)
diversity.plan
@
